  },
  {
    "question": "Is Tesamorelin really FDA-approved?",
    "answer": "Yes. Tesamorelin is FDA-approved under the brand name Egrifta for HIV-associated lipodystrophy. Eden still determines eligibility through consultation and clinical review."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is this the same as Ozempic or other GLP-1s?",
    "answer": "No. Tesamorelin has a different mechanism than GLP-1 medications. For some clients, the conversations are complementary, especially when visceral fat remains a concern."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I take Tesamorelin while on a GLP-1?",
    "answer": "Often, yes, but Eden reviews your full medication list, labs, goals, and prescribing history before recommending whether it belongs in your plan."
  },
  {
    "question": "How long until I notice changes?",
    "answer": "Visceral-fat and body-composition changes usually require a longer timeline. IGF-1 changes may be seen earlier on labs. Individual response varies."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is this safe long term?",
    "answer": "Long-term fit depends on your history, labs, risk factors, and response. Eden monitors eligibility and follow-up rather than treating Tesamorelin like an off-the-shelf product."
  },
  {
    "question": "What labs are needed?",
    "answer": "Your clinician may request IGF-1, metabolic markers, and other labs based on your history. Body-composition imaging may be considered when visceral fat is the lead concern."
  },
  {
    "question": "How is eligibility determined?",
    "answer": "Through clinical consultation, medical history, medication review, and labs when appropriate."
  },
  {
    "question": "Will my insurance cover it?",
    "answer": "Many Eden protocols are paid out of pocket. Pricing and fulfillment details are reviewed during your consultation."
  },
  {
    "question": "What happens after the consult?",
    "answer": "If you are a fit, your clinician designs the protocol, reviews fulfillment, and sets follow-up expectations before you start."
  }
